      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Watauga sits at the headwaters of Boone Lake along the Watauga River, about 18 miles above its junction with the South Fork Holston.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watauga%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The city is part of the Johnson City Metropolitan Statistical Area, a component of the Tri‑Cities region that spans Tennessee and Virginia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watauga%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "William Bean, Tennessee’s first white settler, erected a cabin at Boone Creek’s mouth in 1769, just seven miles downstream from present‑day Watauga.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watauga%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Carter’s Depot housed a water tank, several storage buildings, a telegraph office, and a post office, serving travelers for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watauga%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "During the East Tennessee bridge burnings of 1861, Confederate forces heavily guarded the trestle at Carter’s Depot, deterring its destruction.",
